<worksheet xml:id="sample-worksheet">
  <title>Exact Linear Models</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>Creating and using exact linear models</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Please answer the following questions with your group mates.
      Be prepared to put your solutions on the board.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="exact-linear-tent">
      <introduction>
        <p>
          You are hosting a large multi-day sporting camp and need a place for
          the participants to gather outside.
          You are concerned that it might rain, so you investigate renting a
          tent.
          You call Tent-Rent and they say for the size you want it costs $210
          for the setup and removal plus $23.50 per day to rent the tent.
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If you rent the tent for 3 days how much does it cost?
            for 5 days?
            for 2 weeks?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="solutions">
          <p>
            3 days: <m>210 + 23.50(3) = 280.50</m>.
            5 days: <m>210 + 23.50(5) = 327.50</m>.
            14 days: <m>210 + 23.50(14) = 539.00</m>.
            (All in dollars.)
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the cost <m>C(d)</m> for renting the tent for <m>d</m> days.
            (i.e.
            instead of using 5 or 3 for the number of days, use <m>d</m>.)
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="solutions">
          <p>
            <m>C(d) = 210 + 23.50d</m>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use technology to graph <m>C(d)</m>.
            What is the slope of <m>C(d)</m>?
            What is the vertical intercept of <m>C(d)</m>?
            Roughly copy your graph here:
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="solutions">
          <p>
            The graph is a line through <m>(0, 210)</m> rising 23.50 per day.
            The slope is 23.50 (dollars per day) and the vertical intercept is
            210 (dollars, the setup and removal fee).
          </p>

          <image width="50%">
            <description>
              <p>
                Graph of the cost C(d) in dollars against the number of days d.
                It is a straight line starting at the point (0, 210) on the
                vertical axis and rising steadily to about 915 dollars at 30
                days.
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure label="tent-cost-graph" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="5">
                <definition>
                  f(x) = 210 + 23.5*x
                </definition>
                <coordinates bbox="[-5,-100,38,1100]">
                  <grid-axes xlabel="d (days)" ylabel="C (dollars)"/>
                  <graph at="cost-line" function="f" domain="(0,30)"/>
                  <point p="(0,210)" at="intercept"/>
                  <label anchor="(0,210)" alignment="ne">
                    <m>
                      (0,210)
                    </m>
                  </label>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure" text="Graph of the tent rental cost against the number of days">
                    <annotation ref="cost-line" text="A line rising 23.50 dollars for each additional day"/>
                    <annotation ref="intercept" text="The vertical intercept, 210 dollars, the setup and removal fee"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Is <m>(30,920)</m> on the graph?
            Is <m>(20,680)</m> on the graph?
            Why or why not?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="solutions">
          <p>
            <m>C(30) = 915 \ne 920</m>, so <m>(30,920)</m> is not on the graph.
            <m>C(20) = 680</m>, so <m>(20,680)</m> is on the graph.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose I have $2000 for tent rental.
            How long can I rent the tent?
            First write down an equation you must solve to answer this question.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="solutions">
          <p>
            Solve <m>210 + 23.50d = 2000</m>, which gives
            <m>d = 1790/23.50 \approx 76.17</m>.
            You can rent the tent for 76 full days (cost $1996).
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise xml:id="exact-linear-taxi">
      <introduction>
        <p>
          You have traveled to Chicago and need to get across town.
          You decide to take a taxi.
          The total cost of a ride of <m>m</m> miles is given by
          <me>
            C(m) = 4.50 + 3.25m.
          </me>
        </p>
      </introduction>

      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is the cost of a ride of 5 miles?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="solutions">
          <p>
            <m>C(5) = 4.50 + 3.25(5) = 20.75</m>, so $20.75.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If you only have $30 for taxi fare, how far can you go?
            First write down an equation you must solve to answer this question.
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="solutions">
          <p>
            Solve <m>4.50 + 3.25m = 30</m>, which gives
            <m>m = 25.50/3.25 \approx 7.85</m>.
            You can go about 7.8 miles before reaching $30.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is the slope of <m>C(m)</m>?
            What are the units of this number?
            What does it mean in this context?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="solutions">
          <p>
            The slope is 3.25 dollars per mile: each additional mile costs
            $3.25.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is the vertical intercept of <m>C(m)</m>?
            What are the units of this number?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="solutions">
          <p>
            The vertical intercept is 4.50 dollars, the initial charge (a ride
            of 0 miles).
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            For what value of <m>m</m> does <m>C(m)=30</m>?
            What does this value mean in this context?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="solutions">
          <p>
            <m>m \approx 7.85</m>: a ride of about 7.85 miles costs exactly $30.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>

      <task workspace="0.75in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What is <m>C(7.5)</m>?
            What does this value mean in this context?
          </p>
        </statement>

        <solution component="solutions">
          <p>
            <m>C(7.5) = 4.50 + 3.25(7.5) \approx 28.88</m>: a 7.5-mile ride
            costs about $28.88.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>